WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.730
A university once left a storage bucket of

2
00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:06.330
student records open to the internet.

3
00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.710
No one hacked anything.

4
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.000
A default setting was

5
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.300
simply never changed.

6
00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:16.490
Today: the classic design principles for

7
00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.810
secure systems, how STRIDE finds threats

8
00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.630
before attackers do, and how to teach both

9
00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:24.350
without a list to memorise.

10
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.640
Security aims at three properties.

11
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.420
Confidentiality keeps data

12
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.460
from the wrong people.

13
00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.830
Integrity keeps it from being

14
00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:35.010
changed without permission.

15
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.970
Availability keeps it there when

16
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.390
the right people need it.

17
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.860
In nineteen seventy five, Saltzer

18
00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.400
and Schroeder listed design

19
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.920
principles that still hold.

20
00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.410
Fail-safe defaults mean

21
00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.390
deny unless allowed.

22
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.870
Least privilege means give

23
00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:54.070
only what is needed.

24
00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.560
Complete mediation means

25
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.020
check every access.

26
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:02.050
Industry breach reports repeatedly find

27
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.910
that stolen credentials, phishing and

28
00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:07.030
misconfiguration start most incidents.

29
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.420
Exotic exploits are a small share.

30
00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.510
That is a lesson for what we teach first.

31
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.860
Threat modelling asks what could

32
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.660
go wrong before it does.

33
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.580
Draw the data flows, mark trust

34
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.340
boundaries, and walk each element through

35
00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.900
STRIDE: spoofing, tampering, repudiation,

36
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.060
information disclosure, denial of service

37
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.160
and elevation of privilege.

38
00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.990
Principles taught as a list are

39
00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.310
forgotten by the exam.

40
00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:41.550
Instead, threat-model a system students

41
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.370
use every day, like the university portal.

42
00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.930
Each team draws the data flow and

43
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.190
finds one threat per STRIDE letter.

44
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:51.930
Quick check!

45
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.840
The open bucket was readable by

46
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.860
anyone because access was allowed

47
00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.500
until someone blocked it.

48
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.680
Which principle did it break?

49
00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:04.470
Fail-safe defaults.

50
00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.990
Access should be denied unless

51
00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:08.390
someone grants it on purpose.

52
00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.630
A secure system is safe before

53
00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:13.230
anyone configures it.

54
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.440
In the app, open the breach lab.

55
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.210
Set up the five accounts, let the

56
00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.490
forum get hacked, and name the

57
00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:23.390
principle each defence applies.

58
00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:25.570
Your turn.

59
00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.850
A student edits a hidden form field to

60
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.030
change their grade before it is saved.

61
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.660
Which STRIDE threat is that?

62
00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:35.480
Tampering.

63
00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:39.570
Remember: deny by default, give least

64
00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:43.230
privilege, check every access, and use

65
00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:46.350
STRIDE on a real system before attackers

66
00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:46.690
do.

67
00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:49.910
Try it in your next lecture: give

68
00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.530
teams a one-page data flow of the

69
00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:53.670
course registration system.

70
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.600
Each team must find one threat for every

71
00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.820
STRIDE letter and propose a fix.
